When one promise fails in `Promise.all`, the entire operation fails and returns an object with a `status` of "rejected". In contrast, `Promise.allSettled` will return an array where each promise's result is either resolved or rejected, but it does not reject if all promises are settled.

### Screen evidence
```javascript
const p1 = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  setTimeout(() => resolve('p1'), 2000);
});

const p2 = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  reject('p2');
});

const p3 = new Promise((resolve, resolve) => {
  resolve('p3');
});
```

