To see the difference when one promise fails, you can add a `catch` block to handle the rejected promise in `Promise.all`, or use `Promise.allSettled` and check the results for each promise individually. For example:
```javascript
const results = await Promise.all([p1, p2, p3].map(p => p.catch(e => e)));
// vs
const settled = await Promise.allSettled([p1, p2, p3]);
```
This will allow you to see the difference in how `Promise.all` and `Promise.allSettled` handle a single failed promise.

